00:15:17,420 --> 00:15:19,420
 Um, there's tons of things that could happen here.

134
00:15:19,420 --> 00:15:22,420
 And I'll go ahead and put up a picture on the screen now.

135
00:15:22,420 --> 00:15:25,420
 Um, it's, it's a sort of an alert from the protocol.

136
00:15:25,420 --> 00:15:30,420
 It says for trust and safety and security, there should always be a human in the loop with the ability to deny sampling requests.

137
00:15:30,420 --> 00:15:35,420
 Applications should provide UI that makes it easy and intuitive to review sampling requests.

138
00:15:35,420 --> 00:15:38,420
 Allow users to view and edit prompts before sending.

139
00:15:38,420 --> 00:15:40,420
 Present generated response for review before delivery.

140
00:15:40,420 --> 00:15:43,420
 This is the same sort of thing they put in the tools section.

141
00:15:43,420 --> 00:15:47,420
 And we all know that the user doesn't always approve tools before they are invoked.

142
00:15:47,420 --> 00:15:54,420
 So I definitely think we'll see scenarios where MCP servers are able to just yeet messages back to the client and have those processed.

143
00:15:54,420 --> 00:16:05,420
 And an malicious MCP server may be able to invoke tools on a different, um, MCP server, uh, or incur, you know, negative effect to the victim that way.

144
00:16:05,420 --> 00:16:07,420
 Um, so that's something to be thinking about.

145
00:16:07,420 --> 00:16:11,420
 Uh, I know that sampling and elicitation, which is what we'll cover next are relatively new to the protocol.

146
00:16:11,420 --> 00:16:17,420
 So, um, I think this is something we might be a little bit fast on, but something to be aware of, uh, for sure.

147
00:16:17,420 --> 00:16:26,420
 Um, I also wanted to mention here that even if there is a prompt to have the user approve, you know, the MCP servers modified message or whatever.

